		<id>https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=1835#p1835</id>
		<link href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=1835#p1835"/>
		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Impulse-based dynamic simulation system]]></title>

		
		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=1835#p1835"><![CDATA[
I tried already applying friction impulses at the end of all iterations. The results didn't convince me of this. I had more problems than now. The problem with this approach is that a friction impulse in general causes a change of the normal velocity in the contact point. So the normal velocity after contact resolution is not correct anymore. And since the normal velocity in the contact point is not correct the normal impulse is wrong as well. And so in the end the friction impulse which depends on the normal impulse is not correct. <br><br>It is hard to find a correct solution for this problem. But I can say that I have already made some nice simulations with my method and even mechanical toys like the tippe top or the rattleback worked very well <img class="smilies" src="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/images/smilies/icon_biggrin.gif" width="15" height="15" alt=":D" title="Very Happy">  <br><br>Jan<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=818">Jan Bender</a> — Thu Sep 21, 2006 6:16 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
